WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.176 --> 00:00:07.269
Here are 23 AI agents for the software delivery lifecycle, built as a proof of concept.

2
00:00:07.269 --> 00:00:11.331
They run as LangGraph agents, hosted in Microsoft Foundry.

3
00:00:11.584 --> 00:00:16.211
Now business users can work with all of them, from one friendly chat window.

4
00:00:16.211 --> 00:00:16.576
Lumen.

5
00:00:17.388 --> 00:00:19.109
Here's how it fits together.

6
00:00:19.109 --> 00:00:21.077
Lumen runs on your own computer.

7
00:00:21.077 --> 00:00:27.777
When you pick one of these agents, Lumen sends your request and your attached files to that agent in Foundry.

8
00:00:28.028 --> 00:00:35.431
It signs in with your own Azure account, stores no secrets, and nothing changes in any system until a person approves it.

9
00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:38.193
Setup is one field.

10
00:00:38.193 --> 00:00:41.359
In Settings, paste your Foundry project address.

11
00:00:41.359 --> 00:00:44.129
Lumen checks it, and the agents are ready.

12
00:00:48.109 --> 00:00:53.449
On the Agents page, every one of these agents carries a Microsoft Foundry tag.

13
00:00:53.449 --> 00:00:58.925
Requirements, design, backlog, planning, integration, security and architecture.

14
00:01:03.467 --> 00:01:08.272
Meet Amira, a business analyst on Release 2 of a customer services platform.

15
00:01:08.272 --> 00:01:13.899
She has a new business requirements document, and the functional design starts next week.

16
00:01:15.107 --> 00:01:17.899
She opens the Requirements Refinement agent.

17
00:01:17.899 --> 00:01:20.184
It tells her exactly what to attach.

18
00:01:24.421 --> 00:01:28.239
She attaches the BRD and asks for structured requirements.

19
00:01:33.486 --> 00:01:36.116
The agent is working in Microsoft Foundry.

20
00:01:36.116 --> 00:01:44.131
It splits the document into sections, extracts every requirement with its exact source text, and checks the quality of each one.

21
00:01:46.395 --> 00:01:48.899
A summary, and the numbers at a glance.

22
00:01:48.899 --> 00:01:50.504
Notice the safety checks.

23
00:01:50.504 --> 00:01:54.420
One section of this BRD hides an instruction aimed at the AI.

24
00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:57.630
The agent flagged it, and the platform blocked it.

25
00:01:57.630 --> 00:01:59.941
No requirement was invented from it.

26
00:02:02.918 --> 00:02:09.388
Every requirement has a stable ID, a type, and a priority taken only from the BRD itself.

27
00:02:09.388 --> 00:02:10.406
Never guessed.

28
00:02:11.376 --> 00:02:13.795
She can keep refining in plain words.

29
00:02:13.795 --> 00:02:21.509
"Merge these two requirements." The agent remembers the conversation, and retires the merged ID so it is never reused.

30
00:02:26.688 --> 00:02:28.915
Meet Theo, an integration architect.

31
00:02:28.915 --> 00:02:34.112
He needs an interface control document for the customer platform Name Search lookup.

32
00:02:43.135 --> 00:02:49.685
He attaches the partner contract, the requirements, the canonical model, and a short description of the interface.

33
00:03:00.394 --> 00:03:02.537
Before drafting, the agent pauses.

34
00:03:02.537 --> 00:03:06.192
It wants the architect to confirm the integration pattern.

35
00:03:06.192 --> 00:03:09.973
The decision appears as an approval card, right in the chat.

36
00:03:10.927 --> 00:03:11.802
Theo approves.

37
00:03:11.802 --> 00:03:17.114
Lumen sends his decision back to the agent, and it picks up exactly where it stopped.

38
00:03:20.017 --> 00:03:26.908
A complete draft, checked section by section against the documentation standards, with every gap called out for review.

39
00:03:32.121 --> 00:03:33.611
And meet Priya, the planner.

40
00:03:33.611 --> 00:03:36.643
The steering committee wants to know why the dates moved.

41
00:03:45.296 --> 00:03:52.016
She attaches the integrated plan, exported from Microsoft Project, and asks the Schedule Variance agent.

42
00:04:02.767 --> 00:04:09.362
The numbers are computed in code: baseline against current, slip in working days, the critical path.

43
00:04:09.362 --> 00:04:14.308
The AI only explains them, and every finding is banded red, amber or green.

44
00:04:15.676 --> 00:04:19.459
All 23 agents are live in Foundry today, in practice mode.

45
00:04:19.459 --> 00:04:26.961
They read what you attach, and they never write to Azure DevOps, SharePoint or Teams without a person approving it.

46
00:04:27.873 --> 00:04:29.672
Lumen and Microsoft Foundry.

47
00:04:29.672 --> 00:04:33.270
Delivery agents, made friendly for everyone on the team.
